{"name":"Prudence","hex":"#d4c6db","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Prudence","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A female given name from English, one of the Puritan virtue names."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Prudence"}],"entries":[{"title":"Prudence","blocks":[{"text":"Prudence is the ability to govern and discipline oneself by the use of reason. It is classically considered to be a virtue—in particular, one of the four cardinal virtues of Ancient Greek philosophy and one of the seven heavenly virtues of Christianity. Prudentia is an allegorical female personification of the virtue, whose attributes are a mirror and snake, and who is frequently depicted as a pair with Justitia, the Roman goddess of Justice."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/The_crowned_Prudencia%2C_riding_a_wagon_and_speaking_to_women_Wellcome_L0029370.jpg/330px-The_crowned_Prudencia%2C_riding_a_wagon_and_speaking_to_women_Wellcome_L0029370.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prudence"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Saint-Denis_%2893%29%2C_basilique%2C_monument_fun%C3%A9raire_de_Louis_XII_et_Anne_de_Bretagne%2C_1515%2C_la_Prudence.JPG/500px-Saint-Denis_%2893%29%2C_basilique%2C_monument_fun%C3%A9raire_de_Louis_XII_et_Anne_de_Bretagne%2C_1515%2C_la_Prudence.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Saint-Denis_%2893%29%2C_basilique%2C_monument_fun%C3%A9raire_de_Louis_XII_et_Anne_de_Bretagne%2C_1515%2C_la_Prudence.JPG/1280px-Saint-Denis_%2893%29%2C_basilique%2C_monument_fun%C3%A9raire_de_Louis_XII_et_Anne_de_Bretagne%2C_1515%2C_la_Prudence.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9f/Saint-Denis_%2